===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*temh₁-|*ten-|*-wénts}}
From Late {{inh|en|enm|tempestious}}, {{m|enm|tempestous}}, {{m|enm|tempestuous|t=stormy, turbulent, tempestuous}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=tempestǒus|pos=adj|id=MED44729}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|tempestous}}, and {{der|en|fro|tempesteus}}, {{m|fro|tempestos}}, {{m|fro|tempestous}}, {{m|fro|tempestuose}} (modern {{cog|fr|tempétueux}}), and directly from its {{glossary|etymon}} {{der|en|la|tempestuōsus|t=stormy, turbulent, tempestuous; impetuous}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=adj|id=8277167332|date=June 2024|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj|nodot=1}}; {{R:OED Online|entry=† tempestous|pos=adj|id=6283413656|date=July 2023}}</ref> from {{m|la|tempestās}}, {{m|la|tempestūs|t=point or period of time; season; weather, specifically bad weather; storm, tempest}} (from {{m|la|tempus|t=period of time; (''rare'') weather}}, possibly from {{der|en|ine-pro|*temh₁-|t=to cut}} or {{m|ine-pro|*ten-|t=to extend, stretch}}) + {{m|la|-ōsus|pos={{glossary|suffix}} meaning ‘full of; overly; prone to’ forming {{glossary|adjective}}s from {{glossary|noun}}s}}. The English word is equivalent to {{affix|en|tempest|-uous}} (a variant of {{m|en|-ous|pos=suffix forming adjectives from nouns, denoting the presence of a quality, typically in abundance}}).

